A||||11||My7Fieros||THATS IT!!! Ive had it with the *#^($@ squeeky belt!!!||||8||
Z||000000||My7Fieros||12-11-2000||11:49 PM||||This squeeky belt thing is getting WORSE AND WORSE!!! It does it upon start up for about 30 seconds to a minute..then when I put it in reverse it does it for another minute or so..and at night when I have to turn the lights on..FORGET IT!! It gets really bad! Its now doing it at night when the lights are on EVERY time I stop at a red light...wtf?<BR>Of course it was 19 degrees tonight when I left ...dont dare turn the heater on..itll NEVER shut up then! The belt is new (relativly, about 3 months) and the water pump is new as well..ive tightened it to no avail...its getting VERY VERY annoying! <BR>Also when I turn on the lights, hit the brakes, turn on the heater, whatever, the volt meter drops to 13, and of course, the squeeking begins. Do I need a new altinator? (God I hope not, thats a pain in the a$$ to replace)..but if it works Im all for it..I know this has been posted a zillion times, but Im at my wits end! HELLLPPPPP!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> BTW its an 88 V6<p>[This message has been edited by My7Fieros (edited 12-11-2000).]||||reg||8
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||12-12-2000||12:08 AM||||My '86 did the same thing till I put a star (tooth) washer on the adjusting bolt. It would seem that the lower mount and the bolt were both worn out. New, they both had rough surfaces to 'get a grip' (LOL). The star (tooth) washer cured the problem, I haven't heard a peep since! (Been months now) It's cold, wet and very damp outside when it's not frozen. Still quiet, give it a shot!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||12-12-2000||01:38 AM||||When I first bought my 86 SE it squeeked a lot too. I replaced belt and soon after it started again. I pulled the belt off and turned the pulleys. No problems. Then I hooked the belt back up and with a long metal rod I listened to the water pump and the alternator. The alternator sounded like sand was it in. Replaced alternator. No problems since. It seems the alternator bearing can wear on the edges so it only is bad under torque of the belt.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 12-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Joe4x4||12-12-2000||08:25 AM||||Old trick I used.....get a bar of soap and while car is running .. take bar of soap rub inside belt (( be careful )). <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000004||Tigger||12-12-2000||02:20 PM||||I think Orief is on to something, when you turn your lights on, your adding load the alternator.  If it's been a long time since you've had the alternator might be time to replace it.  The bearings can get sensitive to torque especially with the alt. under load.  The star nut washer is good to do too.<P>Just my viewpoint on what has been said already.||||reg||1
Z||000005||lawrence||12-12-2000||08:10 PM||||A bar of soap, just like belt dressing, is really just addressing the symptom.  If you have a voltmeter in the car, you'll see the voltage is low because the belt is slipping, and probably more with the soap.  It acts like a lubricant, so the noise goes away temporarily.<P>You really need to get a new, cogged belt and ask someone to help you.  Get under the car, and ask your friend to hold the alternator tight against the belt with a crow bar.  Then tighten the bolt that holds the alternator.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||My7Fieros||12-13-2000||03:26 PM||||Good advice all...Im really thinking it may be the altinator, seeing how it really only does it bad when something is turned on (lights, heater, ect.)...Im gonna give it a try...thanx.||||reg||1
Z||000007||ROB MAC||12-14-2000||05:47 PM||||Try a good old fashion SOLID V-belt. Throw those notched, ribbed , cogged , toothed gator back pieces of s#!t away.||||reg||1
Z||000008||sqoach||12-14-2000||08:00 PM||||hmm...just had this problem with my 86 coupe. replaced the water pump not too long ago also. i heard some squealing and thought it might be the a/c belt, so i tightened it, cuz new belts seem to stretch out a bit after you put them on. still squealed. even more when i turned on lights, a/c and such. the alternator belt was tight so i tried some of that belt dressing stuff. by the next day, after allowing that stuff to work, squealing was gone and i was happy   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>well, that's my experience with squealing belts, hope it helps. if not, ask someone that knows what they're talking about   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh<BR>fiero7@hotmail.com<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig_2.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by sqoach (edited 12-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||2birds||12-14-2000||11:24 PM||||I finally cured my squeal by putting a sleeve on the harmonic balancer.   I was losing oil here, and it was getting all over the belt. It only comes back now when my battery has been drained, and it goes away once it gets recharged.<p>[This message has been edited by 2birds (edited 12-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||ezsurf||12-15-2000||12:57 AM||||It sounds like a loose belt.  Make sure the belt is tight.  Use a tentioning tool if you have one, if you don't have one I'd highly recommend getting one.  You can get one from Harbor Freight or JC Whitney.  If the belt is tight, clean your pulleys with carburator cleaner. If that doesn't do it it's probably a bearing in the alternator.||||reg||1
Z||000011||GT Bastard||12-15-2000||01:47 AM||||Fiero God says that if you buy a cheap belt such as a DriveRite that it will be too long. Sometimes even a Gates belt will be a fraction of an inch too long. A new belt will also strech some. Fiero God suggest to use a Dayco belt. Also the star washer idea is not a bad one.||||reg||1
